<DocLayout
  noindex={true}
  title="Content credentials"
  description="How to interpret content credentials in Image Identifier — signature, trust, and what absence means."
  updated="2026-10-14"
>
  <p>
    Some images carry recorded credentials that describe how the file was created or edited. When
    present, Image Identifier surfaces what those records claim and whether basic integrity checks
    passed.
  </p>

  <h2>What you may see</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Credentials found</strong> — the file included readable recorded credentials.</li>
    <li><strong>Signature valid / invalid</strong> — whether cryptographic checks on those records passed.</li>
    <li><strong>Trust confirmed / not confirmed</strong> — whether the signer matched a known trust source used by the check.</li>
    <li>Recorded software, actions, or source relationships when the file includes them.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What this does not mean</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A valid signature does not prove the scene in the photo is truthful.</li>
    <li>A trusted signer is not the same as an authentic image.</li>
    <li>Missing credentials do not prove the image is authentic or camera-captured.</li>
    <li>An invalid signature is not proof the image is AI-generated.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to use it</h2>
  <p>
    Treat credentials as one evidence source. Compare them with file details and any optional
    statistical check. If signals conflict, keep both — do not discard one silently.
  </p>
  <p>
    Try a file in the <a href="/">tool</a>, then open the Credentials tab in your results.
  </p>
</DocLayout>
